<BaseLayout pageId="lawrence-sewer-lateral-repair">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Sewer lateral repair in Lawrence, Indiana</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      In Lawrence, the sewer lateral that runs from your house to the public main is yours — including
      the part under the street right-of-way — and repairing it requires a permit from the City. If the
      repair is done by lining or pipe bursting, the City's current policy also requires camera video of
      the finished work before it is approved.
    </p>

    <EvidenceNote kind="fact">
      <p>
        Every Lawrence statement below is cited to the City's ordinance, the utility's current policy
        manual, or the utility's own homeowner newsletter. Each source, and its limitations, is listed
        under <a href="#sources">sources</a>.
      </p>
    </EvidenceNote>

    <h2 id="what-is-yours">What counts as your pipe</h2>
    <p>
      Lawrence ordinance calls it the <strong>building sewer</strong>: the pipe that runs from your
      building drain to the public sewer. The definition expressly includes the portion of that pipe
      inside the public right-of-way. The building drain itself is treated as ending five feet outside
      the building wall, so almost everything between your foundation and the public main is the
      building sewer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Other words for the same pipe are <em>lateral</em>, <em>private lateral</em>, and
      <em>sewer service line</em>. The utility's newsletter uses "private sanitary sewer lateral" for
      it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="responsibility">Who is responsible for repairs</h2>
    <p>
      The ordinance places responsibility on the property owner who benefits from the connection:
      providing, installing, and connecting it, and making all necessary repairs, extensions,
      relocations, changes, or replacements. The utility's 2023 homeowner newsletter says the same
      thing in plain language — the owner maintains the lateral from the structure to the public main.
    </p>
    <p>
      The ordinance also lets the Director alter, modify, or waive those requirements where compliance
      is not possible because of extenuating circumstances.
    </p>

    <EvidenceNote kind="inference">
      <p>
        The 2019 policy manual describes owner responsibility "from the property line to the point of
        connection with the public main." Read alone, that sentence could be mistaken for a rule that
        the City owns the pipe up to the property line. Read together with the ordinance definition
        (which includes the right-of-way portion) and the newsletter (structure to main), the three
        sources point the same way: the owner is responsible for the full lateral. This reconciliation
        is our reasoning from three consistent sources, not a single statement in one of them.
      </p>
    </EvidenceNote>

    <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
      <p>
        We have not established who is responsible for the <strong>tap or wye at the main itself</strong>,
        how often the Director's waiver is used in practice, or whether any cost-share or assistance
        program exists. We are also not publishing a property-line responsibility diagram until the
        tap question is confirmed with Lawrence Utilities, because a diagram would imply an answer we
        do not have.
      </p>
    </EvidenceNote>

    <h2 id="before-you-hire">Before you hire anyone: find out whose pipe is failing</h2>
    <p>
      The utility's newsletter tells homeowners to call the City's after-hours line first when sewage
      backs up, and it gives a concrete financial reason: if the problem turns out to be in the public
      main, the City does not reimburse what you already paid a contractor. The newsletter also
      describes City crews cleaning mains, running camera inspections on mains and building sewers,
      lining old pipe, and excavating broken pipe.
    </p>
    <p>
      That single call is the cheapest step in this whole process, and it is the one most often skipped.
    </p>
    <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
      <p>
        We are not printing a phone number. The number published in the 2023 newsletter was not
        re-verified, and a wrong number during a backup is worse than none. Use the current contact
        details on the City of Lawrence Utilities website.
      </p>
    </EvidenceNote>

    <h2 id="diagnosis">What a camera inspection settles</h2>
    <p>
      A camera inspection is what turns "the drain keeps backing up" into a decision. It shows where the
      defect is, how deep, how long a section is affected, and whether the pipe still holds its shape —
      which is exactly what determines whether a repair can be done from the inside or has to be dug.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lawrence's policy manual treats camera work as a normal part of lateral work in both directions:
      the City may require a lateral camera inspection, with the video provided, instead of excavating
      to expose the pipe, and it requires video of the finished repair for trenchless methods (below).
    </p>

    <DecisionFlow />

    <p>In sequence, that is:</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>A symptom appears — repeated backups, slow drains, or a finding from a camera.</li>
      <li>Establish whether the failure is in your lateral or in the public main.</li>
      <li>If it is the main, it is the utility's to fix. If it is your lateral, the decision is yours.</li>
      <li>A camera inspection locates the defect, its depth, and how much of the line is affected.</li>
      <li>You and the contractor choose a method: spot repair, full-length lining, pipe bursting, or open-cut replacement.</li>
      <li>The work is permitted, inspected, and — for lining or bursting — verified on video before it is accepted.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="methods">The repair methods you are choosing between</h2>
    <p>
      Lawrence's policy manual states plainly that the utility <strong>does allow</strong> rehabilitation
      of laterals using pipe-bursting and lining technologies with appropriate materials. That matters,
      because trenchless repair is not permitted everywhere, and it is the difference between opening a
      driveway and not opening it.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Repair method comparison">
      <table>
        <caption>
          General descriptions of how each method works. Which one suits your line is decided by the
          camera inspection and the contractor, not by this page.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Method</th>
            <th scope="col">What happens</th>
            <th scope="col">Usually considered when</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does not solve</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Spot or sectional repair</th>
            <td>A short section is repaired, or a sectional liner is set across one defect.</td>
            <td>One identified defect in a line that is otherwise sound.</td>
            <td>Deterioration elsewhere in the same line.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Full-length lining (CIPP)</th>
            <td>A resin-saturated liner is installed inside the existing pipe and cured in place to form a new pipe wall.</td>
            <td>The host pipe is continuous enough to line end to end.</td>
            <td>A collapsed line, or a low spot in the pipe's grade.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Pipe bursting</th>
            <td>A new pipe is pulled along the old path while the old pipe is broken outward.</td>
            <td>The existing pipe cannot be lined, but the path can be reused.</td>
            <td>Situations where the route itself has to change; access pits are still dug.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Open-cut replacement</th>
            <td>The pipe is excavated and replaced.</td>
            <td>Collapse, severe misalignment, or a line that must be re-graded or re-routed.</td>
            <td>Nothing structurally — but surface restoration becomes part of the job.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
      <p>
        The table above describes method mechanics in general terms. This project has not yet added
        engineering-standard citations for them, and it has no Lawrence-specific data on which methods
        local contractors offer, how local lines typically fail, or what any of this costs here. We will
        not publish local prices or failure rates until we have evidence for them.
      </p>
    </EvidenceNote>

    <h2 id="permit">The permit Lawrence requires</h2>
    <p>
      Two separate sources say a permit is required. The ordinance makes it unlawful to repair, modify,
      or connect a building sewer without a valid sanitary sewer connection permit issued by the
      Director. The utility's policy manual requires a permit before repair, replacement, or relocation
      of any lateral connected to the City's sanitary sewer.
    </p>
    <p>What the ordinance says an application has to carry:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the owner's and the contractor's information;</li>
      <li>the premises the work is on;</li>
      <li>plans showing the materials of construction and the installation method.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The policy manual adds a drawing of existing and proposed structures and utilities, plus a
      description of the nature, methods, and materials of the work. In practice a competent contractor
      handles the application, but it is the owner who holds the obligation.
    </p>
    <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
      <p>
        We have not verified the current permit fee, the current forms, or which office issues the
        permit today. The 2019 manual lists a DPW office address that we did not confirm is current.
        Confirm the fee, the form, and the office with Lawrence Utilities before you plan around them.
      </p>
    </EvidenceNote>

    <h2 id="inspection">Inspection, and the video that gets trenchless work approved</h2>
    <p>
      For work done in the open, the ordinance requires at least 24 hours' notice that the sewer work is
      ready for inspection, and the building sewer must be exposed from the foundation to the connection
      so it can be inspected. The Director may waive that inspection, with or without conditions, and has
      a right of entry to inspect.
    </p>
    <p>For lining and pipe bursting, the policy manual sets a different kind of proof:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        the contractor submits camera video of the repair to the utility for the work to be approved;
      </li>
      <li>
        for a full rehabilitation, the video has to cover the whole lateral, from the cleanout near the
        structure to the connection at the public main;
      </li>
      <li>
        for a point repair with a sectional liner, the video has to run from the cleanout to at least two
        feet past the repaired section;
      </li>
      <li>the utility may withhold approval if the video shows a defective repair.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Practically: ask before the job starts who is producing that video and whether its cost is in the
      quote. A quote that omits it is not a complete quote.
    </p>
    <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
      <p>
        The 2019 text specifies delivery on DVD. That is almost certainly not how video is delivered
        today, and we have not verified what format the utility currently accepts.
      </p>
    </EvidenceNote>

    <h2 id="replacement">If the line is replaced rather than rehabilitated</h2>
    <p>The policy manual's installation requirements are specific. Among them:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the owner hires a contractor licensed, bonded, and insured in the City of Lawrence;</li>
      <li>approved materials — SDR35 gasketed pipe, Schedule 40 PVC, or Schedule 80 PVC;</li>
      <li>4-inch or 6-inch pipe, with a minimum 6-inch pipe inside the City right-of-way;</li>
      <li>
        at least 18 inches of cover at the building, and 4 feet beyond ten feet from the building where
        the stub depth allows, with minimum slopes set by pipe size;
      </li>
      <li>
        granular backfill to City standards where the lateral runs in or under a driveway or sidewalk, or
        within five feet of a roadway;
      </li>
      <li>cleanouts 1.5 to 3 feet from the building, at bends, and at least every 100 feet;</li>
      <li>tracer tape or wire;</li>
      <li>no backfilling before the utility's inspection, with 24 hours' notice for that inspection.</li>
    </ul>
    <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
      <p>
        These rules are written for new or replacement installation. How far each one applies to a
        trenchless rehabilitation — the pipe material pulled through a burst, for instance — was not
        confirmed with the utility.
      </p>
    </EvidenceNote>

